Fred Fish, long the definitive software historian of the Amiga
community, passed away on April 20th.

This is a man who really made a difference to the roguelike
community.  In the bad, dark days before the UbiquiNet, the Fred Fish
disk collection were one of the primary ways Amiga users could obtain
freeware.  The disks would be distributed to user groups, who would
make copies which users could then buy for the cost of media.

For those of you with an Amiga, or like playing with emulators, there
is an archive of Fred Fish disks, over a thousad of them, at:
http://ftp.funet.fi/pub/amiga/fish/

A list of contents is at:
http://www.amiga-stuff.com/pd/fish.html

Of note to roguelike fans:
Versions of Hack are on disks 7, 25 and 62.
HackLite (an Amiga-specific variant) on 187 and 799.
Nethack is on 189-190, 460 and 822 (which has 3.1, which is the
beginning of the series that presents the game a we know it today,
with Genhenom, the High Priest of Moloch, the elemental planes +
Astral endgame, and other new features)
AmiOmega is on 320 and 528
Larn is on 63

